{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793373950,"nonce":"RW7lKiucbQXdMvOVuDjX6xJi7PY=","title":"Cloud Sync desktop client: preflight, upload, resume and the sidebar's data","message":"The desktop side that runs the engine in native/sync and talks to the gateway API: a Rust library RayDE's sidebar (4938944) calls, so it runs off the UI thread, reports bytes and phase, cancels and retries. Steps: preflight (scan with the person's selection, validate against the plan's limits and quota, report included files, size, omissions with reasons, notes about history, LFS and submodules, the allocation and the 24-hour expiry, before any upload); begin a sync and take the lease; upload only the chunks the gateway lacks, in bounded parallelism, with resume after a dropped connection or a restart; revalidate each chunk against its hash as it is read and re-scan when a file changed; publish and surface the URL; keep unsaved editor buffers from the editor's own state; never run git operations that change the repository, never read outside the working tree, never log secrets or tokens. States to expose: not synced, checking, uploading with progress, verifying, synced, offline with changes pending, conflict, failed with retry, expiring, expired. Bridge to RayDE: a RayScript or hdll surface for the sidebar, sign-in through the external browser with PKCE (92e2f4e), and tests against the gateway's local emulation. Done when a real repository can be synced from the desktop, killed mid-upload and resumed, and its generation read back.","files":null}]}